{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115059 :question/math-academy-id "e-4414" :question/problem "Find $A^4$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, to find $A^2,$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A^4 = A^2 \\cdot A^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}81 & 0 \\\\ 100 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115763 :question/math-academy-id "q-32319" :question/problem "If $A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$, then $A^{2} =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115814 :question/math-academy-id "q-36314" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, to find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${A}^{4}={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265932 :question/math-academy-id "q-130564" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265933 :question/math-academy-id "q-130567" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265936 :question/math-academy-id "q-130577" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295065 :question/math-academy-id "q-279422" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${B}^{2}.$\n\n| ${B}^{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find ${B}^{2},$ we multiply $B$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295066 :question/math-academy-id "q-279424" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $H=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${H}^{3}.$\n\n| ${H}^{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, to find ${H}^{2},$ we multiply $H$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{2} & =H\\cdot H \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -18 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${H}^{3}={H}^{2}\\cdot H,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{3} & ={H}^{2}\\cdot H \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -18 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}97 & -118 \\\\ 59 & 38\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295068 :question/math-academy-id "q-279430" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $F=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${F}^{3}.$\n\n| ${F}^{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, to find ${F}^{2},$ we multiply $F$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{2} & =F\\cdot F \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${F}^{3}={F}^{2}\\cdot F,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{3} & ={F}^{2}\\cdot F \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 36 \\\\ 18 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295069 :question/math-academy-id "q-279435" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${A}^{4}.$\n\n| ${A}^{4}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, to find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${A}^{4}={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}14 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}146 & -130 \\\\ 65 & -49\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348088 :question/math-academy-id "q-78528" :question/problem "If $Z=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${Z}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, to find ${Z}^{2},$ we multiply $Z$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{2} & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${Z}^{4}={Z}^{2}\\cdot {Z}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{4} & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot {Z}^{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}38 & 18 \\\\ 9 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348089 :question/math-academy-id "q-78529" :question/problem "If $P=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${P}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${P}^{2},$ we multiply $P$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{2} & =P\\cdot P \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365498 :question/math-academy-id "q-279423" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${A}^{3}.$\n\n| ${A}^{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, to find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${A}^{3}={A}^{2}\\cdot A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 & -15 \\\\ 60 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365507 :question/math-academy-id "q-279446" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $E=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${E}^{2}.$\n\n| ${E}^{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $2$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-4$ | {{field-3}} | $-2$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-4}} | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find ${E}^{2},$ we multiply $E$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365518 :question/math-academy-id "q-279448" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${A}^{2}.$\n\n| ${A}^{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | $3$ | $-3$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-4}} | $11$ | {{field-5}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 11 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380083 :question/math-academy-id "q-78340" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388111 :question/math-academy-id "q-130582" :question/problem "If $U=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${U}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, to find ${U}^{2},$ we multiply $U$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{2} & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${U}^{3}={U}^{2}\\cdot U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{3} & ={U}^{2}\\cdot U \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 24 \\\\ -24 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393863 :question/math-academy-id "q-78497" :question/problem "If $F=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${F}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${F}^{2},$ we multiply $F$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{2} & =F\\cdot F \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486379 :question/math-academy-id "q-279420" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${A}^{2}.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${A}^{2}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486381 :question/math-academy-id "q-78338" :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${B}^{2},$ we multiply $B$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486443 :question/math-academy-id "q-279444" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${B}^{2}.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\">${B}^{2}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-5}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To find ${B}^{2},$ we multiply $B$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115230 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115060 :question/math-academy-id "e-4417" :question/problem "Consider the matrix\n\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} .\n$$\n\n\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & =-{X}^{2}+3X+2 \\\\  & =-{X}^{2}+3X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)=-D^2+3D+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate $D^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & =-{D}^{2}+3D+2I \\\\  & =-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 15 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3+2 & 10+15+0 \\\\ 0 & -9-9+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 25 \\\\ 0 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115815 :question/math-academy-id "q-36317" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $f(A)$ if $f(X) = X^{2} + 2X - 3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115954 :question/math-academy-id "q-78348" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $f(C)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-2X-5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-2X-5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-2X-5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(C)={C}^{2}-2C-5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${C}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(C):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(C) & ={C}^{2}-2C-5I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7-2-5 & 4-4-0 \\\\ 6-6-0 & 7-2-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265959 :question/math-academy-id "q-130684" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$  Find $f(D)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-4 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-4I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)={D}^{2}-4I.$ \n\nLet's calculate ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={D}^{2}-4I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-4 & 0+0 \\\\ 0+0 & 4-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295078 :question/math-academy-id "q-279481" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+2X+2$, find $f(A).$\n\n| $f(A)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+2X+2 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+2X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}+2A+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+2A+2I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+4+2 & -5+(-2)+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 & 9+6+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295079 :question/math-academy-id "q-279485" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}-X-2$, find $f(D).$\n\n| $f(D)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-X-2 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-X-2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)={D}^{2}-D-2I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={D}^{2}-D-2I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9-3-2 & -8-(-2)-0 \\\\ 0-0-0 & 1-1-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295080 :question/math-academy-id "q-279493" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+1$, find $f(A).$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$f(A)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}+I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+1 & 0+0 \\\\ 0+0 & -1+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295081 :question/math-academy-id "q-279495" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $H=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+3X+2$, find $f(H).$\n\n| $f(H)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+3X+2 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+3X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(H)={H}^{2}+3H+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${H}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{2} & =H\\cdot H \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(H):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(H) & ={H}^{2}+3H+2I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7+9+2 & -3+(-3)+0 \\\\ 6+6+0 & -2+0+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -6 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295082 :question/math-academy-id "q-279512" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}-2X-3$, find $f(A).$\n\n| $f(A)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-2X-3 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-2X-3I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}-2A-3I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}-2A-3I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14-8-3 & -10-(-4)-0 \\\\ 5-2-0 & -1-2-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295083 :question/math-academy-id "q-279515" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+2X+5$, find $f(A).$\n\n| $f(A)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+2X+5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+2X+5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}+2A+5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+2A+5I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14+6+5 & 5+10+0 \\\\ 1+2+0 & 9+(-4)+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 15 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302133 :question/math-academy-id "q-32321" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $f(A)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-3X+1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-3X+1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-3X+I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}-3A+I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}-3A+I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10-(-6)+1 & 6-18 \\\\ 1-3 & 15-9+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -12 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348102 :question/math-academy-id "q-78579" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $f(B)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-3X+4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-3X+4 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-3X+4I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(B)={B}^{2}-3B+4I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(B):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B) & ={B}^{2}-3B+4I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-3+4 & -3-(-3)+0 \\\\ -9-(-9)+0 & 7-6+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365529 :question/math-academy-id "q-279486" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+X-4$, find $f(B).$\n\n| $f(B)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X-4 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X-4I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(B)={B}^{2}+B-4I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(B):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B) & ={B}^{2}+B-4I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2+0-4 & 6+2-0 \\\\ -3+(-1)-0 & 7+3-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365538 :question/math-academy-id "q-279506" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $E=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+4X-5$, find $f(E).$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$f(E)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+4X-5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+4X-5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(E)={E}^{2}+4E-5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${E}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(E):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(E) & ={E}^{2}+4E-5I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+8-5 & 0+0-0 \\\\ 3+4-0 & 1+4-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366302 :question/math-academy-id "q-78347" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$  Find $f(B)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}+X+3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X+3 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X+3I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(B)={B}^{2}+B+3I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(B):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B) & ={B}^{2}+B+3I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+2+3 & 0+1+0 \\\\ 0+(-4)+0 & 0+(-2)+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366309 :question/math-academy-id "q-78584" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $H=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$  Find $f(H)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}+X+2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X+2 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(H)={H}^{2}+H+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${H}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{2} & =H\\cdot H \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}22 & 4 \\\\ -12 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(H):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(H) & ={H}^{2}+H+2I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}22 & 4 \\\\ -12 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}22 & 4 \\\\ -12 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}22+5+2 & 4+1+0 \\\\ -12-3+0 & -2-1+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}29 & 5 \\\\ -15 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388132 :question/math-academy-id "q-130681" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$  Find $f(A)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-2X-8.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-2X-8 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-2X-8I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}-2A-8I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 10 \\\\ 15 & 22\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}-2A-8I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 10 \\\\ 15 & 22\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 10 \\\\ 15 & 22\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7-2-8 & 10-4+0 \\\\ 15-6+0 & 22-8-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388139 :question/math-academy-id "q-130687" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $f(C)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-X+5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-X+5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-X+5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(C)={C}^{2}-C+5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${C}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(C):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(C) & ={C}^{2}-C+5I \\\\  & =C-C+5I \\\\  & =5I \\\\  & =5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388146 :question/math-academy-id "q-130688" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $K=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $f(K)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-4X+1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-4X+1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-4X+I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(K)={K}^{2}-4K+I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${K}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{2} & =K\\cdot K \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(K):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(K) & ={K}^{2}-4K+I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7-8+1 & 4-4-0 \\\\ 12-12+0 & 7-8+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486363 :question/math-academy-id "q-78583" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$  Find $f(D)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-3X+5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-3X+5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-3X+5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)={D}^{2}-3D+5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={D}^{2}-3D+5I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-3+5 & -3+9+0 \\\\ -1+3+0 & 3+0+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486453 :question/math-academy-id "q-130673" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$  Find $f(A)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}+X-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X-1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X-I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}+A-I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+A-I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+3-1 & 0-2+0 \\\\ 0+4+0 & 1-3-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial"}] :topic/title "Powers of Matrices"}
